  "slug": "edna-diversity-monkfish-759e",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply nanopore sequencing and low-input genotyping to biodiversity inference from museum, field, and minute biological samples.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread in this set is molecular inference of species identity, diversity, and genomic traits from limited or archival material. Nanopore sequencing underpins a barcoding survey of 3,657 European Ulva samples, which identifies 46 species across 30 countries and flags non-indigenous taxa, and a mini-barcoding workflow that recovered COI sequences from 284 of 288 century-old spider museum specimens, adding 53 new BINs to BOLD. Multiple displacement amplification enables WGS and GBS genotyping of single zebra mussel veliger larvae from about 1 ng DNA. Off-target sequencing of 312 spruce bark beetles reveals frequent nematode associates across Europe. A genome size database for 3,539 non-native tetrapods and a reference genome for the Critically Endangered Bermuda skink provide complementary genomic resources. What is new is the extension of these methods to low-input and century-old material.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
